i've lived in hong kong since 1992  and i'm now planning on leaving  hong kong has changed irrevocably it's  not going to go back to  what it was and we've seen  the best days and you know has  [Music]  accentuated that feeling that  this is the right time to go  so this is what i looked like  30-yard years ago it's probably 28 years  ago or so actually young  smart still had hair  and i do remember  the scaffolding on nathan road  with all the neon at night and it was a  very very atmospheric  and it just reminded me so much of blade  runner hong kong was such a  free-wheeling you know  free society people really have no  filter in like  how they express themselves when you  look at the media  as it used to be very kind of vigorous  and you know we are here in the fcc and  this is where i have my first  journalism interview so that this this  place has a certain significance for me  something that i wouldn't forget easily  so this is chunking mansions this is the  first place i came to when i got off the  plane  the place to come or certainly used to  be if you want a fake rolex watch or a  cheap curry or indeed a cheap dormitory  bed  just really hasn't changed at all  so  to my memory anyway  i think one of the striking things about  hong kong is just  how you have these extremes of wealth  you've got chunking mansions  which is  you know one end of the scale and you  literally cross the road and you go to  the most august grand hotel in hong kong  first time i came to the peninsula was  when i was sent here to interview james  tierne a local business figure and  politician  his secretary said to me he'll meet you  in the peninsula and i said where's that  and his secretary laughed at me and said  you're working in hong kong and you  don't know where the peninsula is  so  that was my first visit here i didn't  realize i had been living opposite it  when i after i first arrived in hong  kong but in a quite different  environment  hong kong when i arrived here was in the  middle of a massive economic boom  the city was sort of brimming over with  nervous anxiety that everyone was in a  hurry to make money  to get foreign passports to move out or  you know at least have a root out  well of course there's the handover  itself from 97  you know the sovereignty had changed it  was now part of china again life just  really kind of carried on as normal  quite interesting to see just  you know how the skyline has changed  there's a lot of new buildings it's kind  of interesting that the hong kong  convention and exhibition center over  there  hadn't been built the land that that  stands on was also sea at the time  uh that's where they held the handover  ceremony 25 years ago  i think there was a more of a sense of  possibility when i arrived in 92 you  know things were still kind of growing  fast  china was sort of in some ways still  kind of getting started  and i think as hong kong got richer and  rich and rich obviously and the young  people the younger generation  particularly don't  seem to feel that sense of possibility  that was there 25 30 years ago  the biggest change by far  was the passing under national security  law two years ago  this time you know all the  apprehension about  what might happen after 1997  the things that people feared have  already happened  i see the 25th anniversary as being  you know something quite sombre really i  think for most people in hong kong when  we know the majority of people in hong  kong  supported democracy wanted to see why  the democracy here  and those hopes have been  seemingly  conclusively  crushed now  i don't think that people fundamentally  have changed i think they're the same  and the many things i love about the way  hong kong people are resilience  adaptability  and never say die attitude and also kind  of humor and irreverence as well  i mean i will miss hong kong as many  things i'll miss about hong kong  [Music]
 

